  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "A rotted ship left a perfect outline in the sand. Suffolk, 1939. Twenty-seven metres of oak, gone, every iron rivet still in place.",
        "visual_query": "suffolk england grassy burial mounds",
        "visual_fallback": "english grassland"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Inside it: a war helmet in hundreds of pieces, gold and garnet shoulder clasps, ten nested silver bowls, a lyre, and a purse of thirty-seven gold coins.",
        "visual_query": "anglo saxon gold garnet artifacts",
        "visual_fallback": "gold artifacts"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Every one of those coins came from a different Frankish mint. Someone was buried here like a king. Except there was no body.",
        "visual_query": "ancient gold coins hoard closeup",
        "visual_fallback": "gold coins"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Soil tests did find phosphate, the trace a body leaves. The usual explanation is acid sand that ate the bone; some argue nobody was buried there.",
        "visual_query": "archaeological excavation sandy trench england",
        "visual_fallback": "excavation trench"
      },
      {
        "narration": "The coins date the grave to between 610 and 635. Most scholars think the man in that ship was Raedwald, king of the East Angles.",
        "visual_query": "river deben estuary suffolk coast",
        "visual_fallback": "english estuary"
      }
    ],
